hi I'm Carmen Berlin I'm the author of  let's take this offline it is a book on  service workers it'll teach you  everything you need to know and more  about service workers and making your  web app work in an offline setting it's  really important these days now that  everything is so reliant on the Internet  to provide a fail-safe for people who  lose their internet connection for  whatever reason it could be commuting to  work traveling being in a remote area it  could be a lot of different reasons that  the internet goes out and a service  worker goes a really long way toward  providing a really consistent experience  in your web app so today what we're  gonna do is we're gonna learn how to  write a very first service worker now  before we get started writing service  workers I think it's really important to  think about what happens if you have a  site that doesn't have a service worker  so this particular site is really really  popular unfortunately it doesn't have a  service worker so if we right-click and  go into our inspector and dev tools you  can actually see under application a  list of service workers that a site has  this particular site doesn't have any so  if it were to go offline as you saw  before there's not a lot that is helping  a user out at that point it's a really  unfortunate user experience for somebody  who for whatever reason you know maybe  they're traveling maybe they're in a  remote area again they can't access it  because there are connections in and out  it happens everybody so it's really  important to provide that consistent  experience so over here we have a site  that's actually already offline and as  you can see it's still loaded in and if  you click over into network  and click on one of these requests you  can actually see that this is loading in  from the serviceworker so if we refresh  from serviceworker no problem let's jump  into so code this is our very simple  very first serviceworker so let's step  through it you can see that there are  three functions we've got our on install  our activate and then fetch so this is  derived from the serviceworker life  cycle the life cycle goes in this order  first the browser sees that there is a  serviceworker that needs to be installed  it'll trigger that on install event  this function will handle for it next  the serviceworker will be activated  lastly it'll handle for fetches and in  between times when none of those events  are being triggered then the  serviceworker is idle a couple of quick  things you need to know about a  serviceworker before we get started a  serviceworker has to run over HTTP it  has to have an ssl certificate you can  get an SSL certificate there's services  out there that provide these let's  encrypt is a really great one definitely  recommend doing that this is really  important for production if you're just  messing around trying to get a feel for  service workers there is an exception so  you can run on local hosts you can run a  service worker on localhost and you  don't have to have an SSL certificate  that's really great again if you're just  getting started and you just want to  write some to kind of get a feel for  them keep in mind that that has to be a  local host you can't run from your  filesystem you can't run from an IP  address it has to be a local host so if  you're seeing errors that seem strange  to you that is a good thing to check  so let's look at this code a little bit  closer this install event is what gets  triggered first the browser is going to  see that a service worker is registered  and then trigger this event within this  event we're calling this caches dot open  function that accepts a string parameter  which is going to be the name of the  cache if the cache already exists it'll  go ahead and open it and you can then  access anything within it if it doesn't  exist it'll create it for you that  returns a promise which is our next  function that promise resolves with a  reference to the cache we just opened  within that we're using this add all  function that accepts a array of strings  which are relative paths to files within  this application there's another way you  could do this called cached ad and it  looks like this it's very very similar  the main difference is it only accepts a  single parameter and so you might not  want to use it if you've got a lot of  files that you need to add personally I  generally use at all underneath that  since it's our first Service Worker we  want to go ahead and just let ourselves  know that everything cached successfully  while at all does return a promise it  doesn't resolve with anything so this  isn't really necessary to our  serviceworker it's just something for us  to know that this worked another thing  that I really recommend adding is this  cache method so it can be very difficult  to debug a serviceworker and it's just  really nice for you to know while you're  working on them but this is what went  wrong if something went wrong and this  is what it is so I highly highly  recommend including those  next in the lifecycle is activate so  activate happens right after our install  event is finished you can do a lot with  in and activate event but since it's our  very first one all we're going to do  right now is let ourselves know that our  service worker is successfully activated  and it's ready to go  the last method that we're going to add  to our service worker today is this  fetch method and this is where we're  actually handling for those fetches that  occur within an app so you might have  expected a return statement here but  it's actually really important to use  this event dot respond with method  instead if it not respond with is what  allows you to actually send a custom  response or an on network response  basically back to the fetch request if  you were to return that would never make  it back to your fetch request and that  fetch would actually go ahead and go  over the network which is obviously not  what we want because we're working on a  Service Worker so this particular  function accepts a function parameter we  passing Cash's match so the first thing  we need to do is see if we even have a  matching response to the request  that's what caches stop matches doing it  returns a promise and you're seeing that  right here and you might notice that we  have this response wrapped in an if  block and so that's because if you look  for a request that doesn't have a  matching response that's going to return  undefined so it's really important that  you always wrap that in an if block so  if it is defined we'll go ahead and  return that response and then we want  that else because if it is not defined  we definitely want the user to still get  the data so this is our very first fully  functioning Service Worker there's just  one more thing we need to do  I mentioned that the browser will  register our serviceworker  or it'll see that it needs to be  registered and we're doing that over in  our index dot HTML it's just this tiny  little if block right here so we call  this method navigator dot serviceworker  dot register and then we pass in the  relative path to the serviceworker  generally service workers are gonna live  at the root level of an app and the  other thing I wanted to point out to you  is that navigator dot serviceworker is  while service workers are really widely  supported there are definitely legacy  apps out there that are so running on IE  11 and maybe even earlier versions where  service workers are not supported so  there's really no loss in wrapping  something in an if block like this and  using feature detection I highly highly  highly recommend it and that is it  you've written your very first  serviceworker so real quick if you liked  this video I hope that you head over to  serviceworker book.com and sign up for  my mailing list this book is gonna be  out really really soon and it's gonna  teach you this and then so much more 